    "description": "Mohammad Mosaddegh led Iran’s National Front and became prime minister in 1951 during the struggle over oil nationalization. His government was overthrown in August 1953 through a coup involving Iranian opponents and British and American intelligence services. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Oil nationalization", "Constitutional politics", "National Front", "1953 coup", "Iran–United Kingdom relations", "Iran–United States relations"],
    "status": "deceased",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:41:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:41:15Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mohammad Mosaddegh: Oil Nationalization and the 1953 Coup | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Mosaddegh’s premiership, Iran’s oil dispute, the International Court of Justice ruling, and the evidence concerning the 1953 coup."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "national-front",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "National sovereignty and constitutional government",
        "content": "Mosaddegh led the National Front, a coalition formed in 1949 around oil nationalization and political democratization. He became prime minister in April 1951. The struggle concerned both control of a major resource and the distribution of power within Iran’s political system. [s1]\n\nHis coalition did not remain united. Disagreements over appointments and emergency powers helped turn former allies against him. Britain meanwhile used economic pressure and covert activity to seek his removal. Domestic conflict and foreign intervention developed together; neither can adequately explain the crisis alone. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "oil-and-law",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the international court actually decided",
        "content": "Iran’s nationalization legislation challenged the position of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company, a British company operating under a concession agreement with Iran. The United Kingdom brought the resulting dispute before the International Court of Justice, the United Nations’ principal judicial organ. Iran contested the Court’s authority to hear the case. [s3]\n\nOn 22 July 1952, the Court held that it lacked jurisdiction. Its reasoning concerned the scope of Iran’s acceptance of compulsory jurisdiction and the treaties on which Britain relied. It also rejected treating the company’s concession agreement as a treaty between Iran and Britain: Britain was not a party to that contract. This was a jurisdictional decision, not a judgment resolving every substantive question about nationalization, compensation, or the parties’ conduct. The distinction matters when describing Mosaddegh’s legal success. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coup-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A coup with foreign planners and Iranian participants",
        "content": "The original coup attempt failed in mid-August 1953, and the shah left Iran. On 19 August, military defections and attacks on government positions brought down Mosaddegh’s administration. General Fazlollah Zahedi replaced him. Mosaddegh subsequently received a prison sentence and remained under house arrest after his release, until his death in 1967. [s1]\n\nThe foreign role rests on documentary evidence, including an internal CIA history discussed and published by the National Security Archive, an independent research organization based at George Washington University. Its collection identifies American and British planning and records Iranian participants. These materials support describing the overthrow as a foreign-backed coup while retaining the agency of Iranian military officers and political actors. [s2]\n\nThe documentary record also has limits. Classification, redaction, and the destruction of records have complicated reconstruction. An intelligence service’s retrospective account should be evaluated as a source produced by a participant, rather than treated as a complete or disinterested explanation. The Archive’s presentation is particularly useful for understanding why the history of the coup also involves a history of disclosure. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "crisis-sequence",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The premiership and its aftermath",
        "content": "```mermaid\ntimeline\n  title Mosaddegh and the oil crisis\n  April 1951 : Becomes prime minister\n  1951 : Oil nationalization legislation\n  July 1952 : International Court finds no jurisdiction\n  August 1953 : Government overthrown\n  1967 : Dies after years under house arrest\n```",
        "summary": "The sequence separates the oil legislation, the Court’s jurisdictional ruling, and the government’s overthrow. Chronological order alone does not explain causation.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-legacy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the legacy through evidence",
        "content": "Mosaddegh’s premiership brings constitutional politics, resource sovereignty, and covert intervention into the same historical frame. A careful reading distinguishes what Iranian legislation enacted, what a court decided, and what later documentation establishes about the overthrow. Those are related questions, but each requires its own evidence. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
